{
  "dom_id": "step-121766",
  "name": "Projecting Perpendicular Vectors",
  "problem": "Notice that if two vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are perpendicular, then by projecting $\\mathbf{a}$ onto $\\mathbf{b}$ (or $\\mathbf{b}$ onto $\\mathbf{a}$), we obtain the zero vector.\n\nFor example, the projection of $\\mathbf{a}$ onto a perpendicular vector $\\mathbf{b}$ looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14146000/assets/9163eda36b752f1e36195b8b5a1d40bf545ec21a3988f5480d6ddcbb926c00ea.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following fact:\n\n*If $\\mathbf{a}$ is a nonzero vector, its projection onto another nonzero vector $\\mathbf{b}$ equals the zero vector if and only if the vectors are perpendicular.*",
  "worked_solution": "",
